This dataframe contains a row for each (character, month) in which the character
was active in the previous month, up until the second-to-last month of the dataset. 
In each row, `has_played` informs us of whether the character logged in at any time in
that month, and `churn_next` is the target that we want to predict on a monthly basis.

::: {.callout-note}
#### Why only characters that were active in the previous month

Features for month M are built from month M-1, so a character that was already absent in 
M-1 has no features at all. Such a character is also very likely to be labeled as 
churned: in the 2008 data, **75%** of the `churn_next=True` rows belong to characters that
were already absent in the previous month, and the trivial rule "absent last month" 
alone reaches a ROC AUC of 0.63. These rows have no features of their own, they 
make up most of the training set, and they are not characters we could still retain. 
We therefore only keep the rows where the character 
played in M-1, which leaves 81,199 of the 221,237 rows, and `churn_next=True` for 36.5% 
of them instead of 54.5%. In other words, the target is now "a character that was active in M-1 and is absent
in both M and M+1".
:::

## Setting up the ML pipeline
Before starting with feature engineering and actually training the model, it's 
worth spending some time understanding the scenario we are in. 

The main factor that complicates this scenario is the fact that we are dealing
with sequential data points, which are therefore not independent of each other: 
*the position of a player at a time T is strongly correlated with their position
at time T+1*. 

### Building a custom splitter
For this reason, we need to use a splitter that returns sequential splits. A 
common example of this is scikit-learn's
[TimeSeriesSplit](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.model_selection.TimeSeriesSplit.html). `TimeSeriesSplit` works by splitting the entire dataset in a given number 
of splits; during cross-validation, at step K the splitter returns the first k
splits as the train split, and the k+1 split as the validation split. 

![](images/timeseriessplit.png){fig-align="center"}

[Source](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/auto_examples/model_selection/plot_cv_indices.html#sphx-glr-auto-examples-model-selection-plot-cv-indices-py)

A downside of `TimeSeriesSplit` is that the splitter assumes uniformly distributed
samples, so that each singular split has the same size as the others. This is a 
problem for our scenario: our samples are (character, month) pairs, and the number
of characters in each month is never constant (in 2008, between about 6,700 and 12,500
rows per month): 

![](images/user_months_plot.png){fig-align="center"}

For this reason, we need to define a custom splitter that is aware of the constraints
we are working with. Given that we are working month-by-month, each split should
contain all the rows of a specific month (the characters that played in the previous
month, as explained above), and should be able to do so regardless of the fact that 
the number of characters changes.

There is a second constraint: the target of a row is the churn status of the 
*following* month, so it is only known one month later. If the training set included 
the rows of the month right before the test month, their targets would be computed 
from the activity in the test month. The splitter therefore leaves a one-month gap 
between the last training month and the test month.

This is what we implement here: 

::: {#436e20a5 .cell execution_count=13}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display .cell-output-markdown}
The boundaries of the experiment are defined in `main.py`: split points go from 2006-02-01 to 2008-10-01, and each test month is the one following its split point. The last test month is the latest one whose target is fully observed.
:::
:::


::: {#be8a4f4e .cell execution_count=14}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display .cell-output-markdown}
```python
class Splitter:
    """
    Iterates over the months. For each split point, the test month is the
    following month: it is the month during which we would score users and act
    on the ones marked as "churn risks".

    The target of a row (churn_next) is the churn status of the month after it,
    so it is only known one month later. The training set therefore stops one
    month before the split point: a row from the split point month would have a
    target computed from the test month.
    """

    def split(self, user_month, has_played=None, interval="1mo"):
        # the target is not needed in this splitter since we are only splitting
        # based on the month
        del has_played
        time_range = pl.date_range(MIN_DATE, MAX_DATE, interval, eager=True)
        for split_point in time_range:
            # I can either use dateutils.relative delta
            # test_month = split_point + relativedelta(months=1)
            # Or do this with polars which is more consistent with the rest of the code
            test_month = pl.Series([split_point]).dt.offset_by(interval).first()
            last_train_month = (
                pl.Series([split_point]).dt.offset_by("-" + interval).first()
            )
            # Train indices are up to last_train_month, included
            train_idx = (
                user_month.with_row_index("idx")
                .filter(pl.col("month") <= last_train_month)["idx"]
                .to_list()
            )
            # Test indices are for the month after split_point
            test_idx = (
                user_month.with_row_index("idx")
                .filter(pl.col("month") == test_month)["idx"]
                .to_list()
            )
            if train_idx and test_idx:
                print(f"Working on month {split_point}")
                yield train_idx, test_idx

    def get_n_splits(self, X, y):
        return sum(1 for _ in self.split(X, y))

```
:::
:::


Like all other scikit-learn splitters, our splitter is a generator that, given 
X and y (`user_month` and the target in our case), returns the indices of the 
samples that should be used in each split. 

## Building the pipeline

### Generating the features
The features of a row at month M are computed from the activity of month M-1 only. 
They fall in a few groups:

- fixed attributes of the character: race and class;
- **session features**: skrub's
  [SessionEncoder](https://skrub-data.org/dev/reference/generated/skrub.SessionEncoder.html)
  groups consecutive heartbeats of a 
  character into sessions, which gives the number of sessions in the month, and the 
  total, average and standard deviation of their duration, and the average start 
  and end time;
- **player features**: highest level, number of distinct zones and guilds, most 
  frequent zone, first and last guild;
- **playerbase features**: average level and number of active players of the 
  character's class;
- optionally, **location features**: how rare the zones visited by the character 
  are, how much time is spent in "hub" zones that everyone visits, and how evenly 
  the time is spread across zones (Gini coefficient);
- **lagged features**: the value of every feature one or more months earlier, 
  plus the month-to-month difference of numeric features, so that the model can 
  see trends.

The functions that compute them live in 
[adding_features.py](https://github.com/skrub-data/wowah-sessionization/blob/main/adding_features.py).
The function below loops over the months, and for each one filters the historical 
data down to the previous month before building the features. Because lagged features 
need each character's complete timeline, the feature table is built once for every 
(character, month) pair, independently of the cross-validation split, and each split 
only selects its rows from it.

{{< include sections/sessionization.qmd >>}}


::: {.callout collapse="true" }
#### Full feature engineering function (click to expand)

::: {#f12306bf .cell execution_count=15}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display .cell-output-markdown}
```python
def filter_df_by_month(df, month):
    return df.filter(pl.col("month") == month)


def build_feature_table(
    historical_data,
    session_gap=30,
    use_location=True,
    add_gini=False,
    interval="1mo",
    lags=(1, 2),
):
    """
    Build the feature table for every (char, month) pair.

    This function makes sure that we are only ever using historical data up to
    the given month - 1 month. This is to avoid any leakage in the data.

    It builds the table for every (char, month) pair a character could appear
    in (not just the rows asked for by a given CV fold), because lagged
    features need each character's full monthly timeline: a CV fold's X may
    contain a single month (e.g. the test fold), which would otherwise leave
    every lag/diff column null.
    """
    features_by_month = []

    # Create a session encoder. The gap is a hyperparameter that can be tuned.
    # This encoder is used as a stateless transformer so it is refitted for every
    # month
    session_encoder = SessionEncoder(
        split_by="char", timestamp_col="timestamp", session_gap=session_gap * 60
    )
    historical_data = historical_data.with_columns(
        month=pl.col("timestamp").dt.truncate(interval)
    ).lazy()

    # Every (char, month) pair a character could appear in, whether or not they
    # played that month: the full grid lag/diff features are built over.
    user_month = build_churn_dataset(historical_data).select("char", "month").lazy()
    # Adding fixed features: these features are fixed by character so they don't
    # change over time.
    user_month = user_month.join(
        historical_data.select("char", "race", "charclass").unique("char"),
        on="char",
        how="left",
        maintain_order="left",
    ).collect()

    # This is used to add the historical data up to the given month.
    # Sorting months is not needed, but forces a consistent order (better for debugging)
    for month in user_month["month"].unique().sort():
        this_month_X = filter_df_by_month(user_month, month)

        # Selecting only the entries in the historical data from the month before
        # the one I am building features for.
        # This means that if the row's month is April, then the historical data
        # is filtered to keep only the rows from March. Shifting the data forward
        # by one interval and filtering on the current month is equivalent to
        # saying "I want the rows for the current month - 1 month", but it's
        # easier to implement
        kept_historical_data = historical_data.with_columns(
            pl.col("month").dt.offset_by(interval)
        ).filter(pl.col("month") == month)

        # Session features: a session starts from a heartbeat, then it ends when
        # no more heartbeats are detected for session_gap minutes
        historical_data_sessions = session_encoder.fit_transform(
            kept_historical_data.collect()
        ).lazy()
        historical_data_sessions = get_session_duration(historical_data_sessions)

        # General features: add session based and playerbase features
        df_with_features = add_general_features(
            this_month_X.lazy(), historical_data_sessions
        )

        # Location features can be useful but take much longer to generate
        if use_location:
            # Grouping by character and zone so that I can get the time spent in each zone
            # Even if users leave the zone, this lets me find how much time a user spends in
            # a given zone
            session_encoder_zone = SessionEncoder(
                split_by=["char", "zone"],
                timestamp_col="timestamp",
                session_gap=session_gap * 60,
                suffix="session_id_zone",
            )
            # Zone-session features: a session lasts from the first time a character
            # enters a zone to the moment it leaves it
            # This is useful to get zone-specific features
            historical_data_zone_sessions = session_encoder_zone.fit_transform(
                kept_historical_data.collect()
            ).lazy()
            historical_data_zone_sessions = get_session_duration(
                historical_data_zone_sessions,
                session_column="timestamp_session_id_zone",
            )
            df_with_features = add_location_features(
                df_with_features,
                historical_data_zone_sessions,
                add_gini=add_gini,
            )
        else:
            df_with_features = df_with_features.collect()
        features_by_month.append(df_with_features)
        assert len(df_with_features) == len(this_month_X)

    feature_table = pl.concat(features_by_month, how="vertical")
    to_fill = pl.col("monthly_total_session_duration", "monthly_avg_session_duration")
    feature_table = feature_table.with_columns(
        to_fill.fill_null(pl.duration(seconds=0))
    )
    if lags is not None:
        feature_table = add_lagged_features(feature_table, lags=lags)
    return feature_table


def add_features(X, feature_table):
    """Attach this CV fold's (char, month) rows to the precomputed features."""
    return X.join(
        feature_table, on=["char", "month"], how="left", maintain_order="left"
    )

